Furthermore, other auspicious omens appeared: a rain of exquisite and highly fragrant flowers fell; the stars and constellations shone even while the sun was bright; extremely clear water seeped out from the ground; burrowing animals emerged from their dwellings; greed, hatred, and delusion did not arise; no dust rose from the ground; foul odors vanished; divine perfumes became fragrant; humans could clearly see the Rūpa-brahmas with their natural eyes; and there was no passing away or rebirth of beings. The occurrence of these omens served as precursors for obtaining the remaining attributes and virtues of the Buddha.
## The Seven Co-natals (Sahajāta)
At the very moment the Bodhisatta was born, the following seven also came into existence simultaneously:
1. Princess Yasodharā, the mother of Rāhula, also known as Bhaddakaccānā;
2. Prince Ānanda;
3. Channa, the courtier;
4. Kāḷudāyī, the courtier;
5. Kaṇṭhaka, the horse;
6. The Mahābodhi, the Bodhi tree;
7. The four great pots of gold.
These seven were born or appeared at the same time as the Great Bodhisatta as his co-natals (sahajāta).

