## The Five Omens that Appear when Devas are about to Pass Away
The five omens (pubbanimitta) are:
(1) The celestial flowers wither;
(2) The celestial garments become soiled;
(3) Sweat exudes from the armpits;
(4) The physical appearance fades;
(5) One no longer delights in one's own celestial mansion.

Among these five:
(1) The withering of celestial flowers means that the celestial flowers that came into existence as ornaments on the day of rebirth (the day of becoming a deva) begin to wither. These celestial flowers appear on the bodies of those devas as soon as they attain celestial life and never wither throughout their entire celestial lifespan, remaining always fresh and vibrant. They only wither when there are only seven days (by human reckoning) of life remaining before passing away from that celestial life. (In the case of the Bodhisatta Setaketu Deva, those celestial flowers never once withered throughout the entire lifespan of the Tusita devas—a period of 576,000,000 years by human reckoning—but they withered seven days before he was to pass away from that celestial life.)

(2) Regarding the soiling of celestial garments, in the same manner as before, the celestial garments never become soiled throughout the entire celestial lifespan, but they become dreadfully soiled only seven days before passing away.

(3) Regarding sweat exuding from the armpits, although there is never any intense heat or cold due to seasonal changes in that celestial realm as there is in the human world, beads of sweat exude and flow from the limbs (especially the armpits) only seven days before passing away.
